WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.690
Our school has six tablets

2
00:00:02.690 --> 00:00:04.710
for nine hundred pupils.

3
00:00:05.590 --> 00:00:07.790
Some colleagues say: then we

4
00:00:07.790 --> 00:00:08.730
can’t teach computing.

5
00:00:09.670 --> 00:00:12.270
But the big ideas of computing

6
00:00:12.270 --> 00:00:14.070
need no device at all.

7
00:00:15.260 --> 00:00:18.000
Today: unplugged lessons that teach real

8
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:20.160
computing, and how to connect them to

9
00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:20.400
code.

10
00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:24.260
Unplugged means teaching computing ideas

11
00:00:24.260 --> 00:00:27.860
with cards, string, chalk and people.

12
00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:31.100
CS Unplugged, a free collection from New

13
00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:33.280
Zealand, has dozens of activities.

14
00:00:34.120 --> 00:00:37.300
Sorting networks, binary cards, searching

15
00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:39.800
games, and error-detecting card tricks.

16
00:00:40.990 --> 00:00:42.090
Here’s a favourite.

17
00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:46.260
Five pupils hold cards with one, two,

18
00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:49.540
four, eight and sixteen dots.

19
00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:52.820
Each card is face up or face

20
00:00:52.820 --> 00:00:56.820
down: on or off, one or zero.

21
00:00:57.490 --> 00:00:59.230
To make thirteen, show

22
00:00:59.230 --> 00:01:01.570
eight, four and one.

23
00:01:02.170 --> 00:01:03.950
Five cards make every

24
00:01:03.950 --> 00:01:05.250
number up to thirty-one.

25
00:01:06.470 --> 00:01:08.410
Unplugged works best when it’s

26
00:01:08.410 --> 00:01:10.030
linked to code soon after.

27
00:01:10.690 --> 00:01:12.690
After the binary cards, show

28
00:01:12.690 --> 00:01:14.310
Python doing the same thing.

29
00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:18.940
bin of thirteen gives one, one, zero, one.

30
00:01:19.570 --> 00:01:22.170
The idea came first; now

31
00:01:22.170 --> 00:01:23.950
pupils see a computer do it.

32
00:01:25.110 --> 00:01:26.630
Unplugged has limits.

33
00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:30.080
It teaches concepts, but not the habits

34
00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:32.280
of writing and debugging real code.

35
00:01:32.990 --> 00:01:35.410
Research suggests it works best as a

36
00:01:35.410 --> 00:01:37.830
start, not instead of programming.

37
00:01:38.620 --> 00:01:40.940
So plan the plugged part, even

38
00:01:40.940 --> 00:01:42.520
with one phone for the class.

39
00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:44.340
Quick check!

40
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:47.440
Which of these teaches a computing idea

41
00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:50.580
with no device: pupils sorting themselves

42
00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:53.540
by height in a chalk sorting network, or

43
00:01:53.540 --> 00:01:55.160
watching a video about computers?

44
00:01:56.540 --> 00:01:57.460
The sorting network.

45
00:01:58.270 --> 00:02:00.290
Pupils walk along chalk lines,

46
00:02:00.650 --> 00:02:02.210
compare numbers at each meeting

47
00:02:02.210 --> 00:02:04.130
point, and swap if needed.

48
00:02:04.780 --> 00:02:06.940
They’re acting out a real algorithm,

49
00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:09.240
and they can feel why it works.

50
00:02:10.610 --> 00:02:12.790
Choose activities by the idea they

51
00:02:12.790 --> 00:02:14.830
teach, not by how fun they look.

52
00:02:15.410 --> 00:02:17.310
Human robot for algorithms.

53
00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:19.620
Binary cards for data.

54
00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:21.980
Guess my number for searching.

55
00:02:22.700 --> 00:02:24.480
A card trick for detecting errors.

56
00:02:25.260 --> 00:02:28.660
Each one needs only paper, chalk or cards.

57
00:02:30.030 --> 00:02:32.170
A good unplugged lesson has a shape.

58
00:02:32.830 --> 00:02:34.050
Start with a puzzle.

59
00:02:34.750 --> 00:02:36.250
Let pupils act it out.

60
00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:39.380
Name the idea: this is binary,

61
00:02:40.060 --> 00:02:41.020
this is an algorithm.

62
00:02:41.750 --> 00:02:43.770
Then show it in code, on one

63
00:02:43.770 --> 00:02:45.250
phone if that’s all you have.

64
00:02:46.510 --> 00:02:48.730
In the app, open Bits and bytes.

65
00:02:49.550 --> 00:02:52.090
Flip eight switches to make any number

66
00:02:52.090 --> 00:02:54.410
from zero to two hundred and fifty-five,

67
00:02:54.410 --> 00:02:58.190
then make sixty-five and see the letter A.

68
00:02:58.760 --> 00:03:01.040
It’s the binary cards, plugged in.

69
00:03:02.250 --> 00:03:02.770
Your turn.

70
00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:05.180
Your class has just played

71
00:03:05.180 --> 00:03:06.920
the binary cards game.

72
00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:09.240
What’s the best next step?

73
00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:11.380
Link it to code.

74
00:03:12.110 --> 00:03:14.990
Remember: unplugged teaches real ideas

75
00:03:14.990 --> 00:03:17.350
with cards, chalk and people.

76
00:03:17.970 --> 00:03:20.550
Choose activities by the idea they teach.

77
00:03:21.080 --> 00:03:23.800
Give each lesson a shape: puzzle,

78
00:03:24.200 --> 00:03:26.740
act it out, name it, code it.

79
00:03:27.320 --> 00:03:29.780
And it’s a start, not a replacement.

80
00:03:31.110 --> 00:03:33.570
Try it in class tomorrow: binary cards.

81
00:03:34.330 --> 00:03:37.570
Make five cards with one, two,

82
00:03:38.030 --> 00:03:41.110
four, eight and sixteen dots.

83
00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:43.940
Five pupils show numbers

84
00:03:43.940 --> 00:03:45.300
the class calls out.

85
00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:48.180
End by showing bin of a

86
00:03:48.180 --> 00:03:49.040
number on your phone.
